To find the travel time from the astronaut’s point of view, we need the proper time:

\[
\tau = \frac{t}{\gamma}
\]

where \(t\) is the travel time in Earth’s frame and \(\gamma\) is the Lorentz factor.

The Large Magellanic Cloud is about \(160{,}000\) light-years from Earth. At speed

\[
v = 0.99999987c
\]

the Earth-frame travel time is approximately

\[
t \approx \frac{160{,}000\ \text{ly}}{0.99999987c} \approx 160{,}000\ \text{years}
\]

The Lorentz factor is

\[
\gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}
\]

For \(v = 0.99999987c\),

\[
\gamma \approx 1960
\]

Thus the astronaut’s elapsed time is

\[
\tau \approx \frac{160{,}000}{1960} \approx 81.6\ \text{years}
\]

So the astronaut experiences about 81 years of travel time. Since the astronaut starts at age 22 and the aliens live about 150 years, the astronaut would arrive at about age 103, so they do not die before reaching Earth.

Answer: B
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

